{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P52bfQ7GbsATs1VMkQzvPN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CODATA silicon lattice parameter a 5.431020511 Å 22.5 °C NIST\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing\"},{\"title\":\"Template:Physical constants\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst\"},{\"title\":\"Loading AI tools\",\"url\":\"https://wikiwand.com/en/Lattice_constants\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/691991\"},{\"title\":\"www.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/node/697111\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2002\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2002.pdf\"}]\n\nThe value 5.431020511 Å (5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m) is the CODATA lattice parameter of silicon, and it is measured at 22.5 °C in vacuum.\n\n- **NIST 2018 X-ray table:** It lists the lattice parameter of Si at 22.5 °C in vacuum as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸. It also gives the {220} lattice spacing as 1.920 155 716(32) × 10⁻¹⁰ m. (NIST 2018 CODATA X-ray values table)\n- **Ideal crystal definition:** The NIST footnote says the value is for an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si, free of impurities and imperfections. (This is the lattice parameter (unit cell edge length) of an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections.)\n- **CODATA 2022:** The Template:Physical constants page reports the same value, a = 5.431020511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with relative uncertainty 1.6 × 10⁻⁸, and says it was updated to CODATA 2022. (The values have been updated to the CODATA 2022 values.) The CODATA 2022 report on arXiv has a dedicated section on silicon lattice spacings, which I did not read in full. (In this section, we summarize efforts to determine the lattice spacing of an ideal (or nearly perfect) natural-silicon single crystal.)\n\nThe search did not directly show the NIST CODATA 2022 page itself, so the 2022 confirmation comes from a secondary Wikipedia template. The 2018 NIST table is the primary source here.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e00040a-3162-495e-9b5a-0a2f05274bea","uuid":"98f970ae-eb1e-4177-a43b-58b25581c455","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:49:56.142Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CODATA silicon lattice parameter a 5.431020511 Å 22.5 °C NIST","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01ALvBbaaQT5Wf4i8LnzT7Jq","content":[{"title":"CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.03787"},{"title":"Lattice constant","url":"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing"},{"title":"Template:Physical constants","url":"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst"},{"title":"Loading AI tools","url":"https://wikiwand.com/en/Lattice_constants"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/731856"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/691991"},{"title":"www.nist.gov","url":"https://www.nist.gov/node/697111"},{"title":"xray 2002","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2002.pdf"}]},"The value 5.431020511 Å (5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m) is the CODATA lattice parameter of silicon, and it is measured at 22.5 °C in vacuum.\n\n- **NIST 2018 X-ray table:** It lists the lattice parameter of Si at 22.5 °C in vacuum as 5.431 020 511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with a relative standard uncertainty of 1.6 × 10⁻⁸. It also gives the {220} lattice spacing as 1.920 155 716(32) × 10⁻¹⁰ m. (NIST 2018 CODATA X-ray values table)\n- **Ideal crystal definition:** The NIST footnote says the value is for an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si, free of impurities and imperfections. (This is the lattice parameter (unit cell edge length) of an ideal single crystal of naturally occurring Si free of impurities and imperfections.)\n- **CODATA 2022:** The Template:Physical constants page reports the same value, a = 5.431020511(89) × 10⁻¹⁰ m, with relative uncertainty 1.6 × 10⁻⁸, and says it was updated to CODATA 2022. (The values have been updated to the CODATA 2022 values.) The CODATA 2022 report on arXiv has a dedicated section on silicon lattice spacings, which I did not read in full. (In this section, we summarize efforts to determine the lattice spacing of an ideal (or nearly perfect) natural-silicon single crystal.)\n\nThe search did not directly show the NIST CODATA 2022 page itself, so the 2022 confirmation comes from a secondary Wikipedia template. The 2018 NIST table is the primary source here."],"durationSeconds":5.139162279999989,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P52bfQ7GbsATs1VMkQzvPN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q7qw5R6x2hkEfWmSfwWvAs","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Haas Tran Blaha 2009 lattice constants PBE Si Ge C Al Cu zero-point corrected experimental table\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"On the accuracy of commonly used density functional approximations in determining the elastic constants of insulators and semiconductors\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1505.02379\"},{\"title\":\"Assessing the Performance of Recent Density Functionals for Bulk Solids\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0903.4037\"},{\"title\":\"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice dynamics and structural phase stability of group-IV elemental solids with the r$^2$SCAN functional\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11177\"},{\"title\":\"Self-consistent Hessian-level meta-generalized gradient approximation\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.07046\"},{\"title\":\"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z\",\"url\":\"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals\",\"url\":\"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529\"},{\"title\":\"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals\"},{\"title\":\"dft pernot2015 jp509980w supp\",\"url\":\"https://iris.unito.it/retrieve/e27ce428-98c2-2581-e053-d805fe0acbaa/dft_pernot2015_jp509980w_supp.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3331871\"}]\n\nI couldn't retrieve the original 2009 Haas, Tran, and Blaha table itself, so the numbers below come from later papers that reuse or compare against it.\n\n- **Methodology:** A summary of the paper says the experimental lattice constants were zero-point anharmonic expansion (ZPAE) corrected before the statistical analysis Experimental lattice constants were ZPAE - corrected, and statistical quantities like mean error, mean absolute error, etc., were used for result analysis.\n- **Al and Cu:** The summary notes that for Al, most functionals except PBE yield accurate lattice constants. I did not find the specific Al or Cu PBE values from the 2009 table.\n- **Si, Ge, C (ZPE-corrected experiment vs. PBE):** A 2026 r²SCAN paper lists PBE values of 3.571 Å (C), 5.472 Å (Si), and 5.765 Å (Ge), alongside an \"ExptZPE\" column of 3.553, 5.421, and 5.644 Å. Its uncorrected experimental values are 3.567, 5.430, and 5.652 Å. | С | 3.571 | 3.558 | 3.551 | 3.545 | 3.553 | 3.567 | | Si | 5.472 | 5.447 | 5.438 | 5.442 | 5.421 | 5.430 | | Ge | 5.765 | 5.689 | 5.678 | 5.686 | 5.644 | 5.652 | This paper's PBE and experimental values may not match the 2009 table exactly.\n- **Al and Cu (PBE vs. experiment):** A 2026 Hessian-level meta-GGA paper lists PBE at 4.042 Å for Al and 3.642 Å for Cu, against experimental values of 4.018 Å and 3.595 Å. | Al | 4.042 | 4.019 | 4.049 | 4.067 | 4.011 | 4.052 | 4.018 | Its experimental reference may differ from the one used in 2009.\n- **Other reference data:** A 2009 Csonka et al. paper reports ZPAE-corrected experimental values, including Si 5.403 Å and Ge 5.623 Å, and it cites the Haas paper's data via its low-temperature and ZPAE-corrected sources. The rest are based on room temperature values from Ref. 60 (C, Si, SiC, Ge, GaAs, NaF, LiF, MgO) and Ref. 57 (LiCl), corrected to the T = 0 limit using the thermal expansion from Ref. 58.\n\nFor the exact 2009 table, the original paper in Phys. Rev. B 79, 085104 is the best source.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e00040a-3162-495e-9b5a-0a2f05274bea","uuid":"271ff0e4-dd3d-4801-88f5-638710fb54e3","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:49:57.967Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Haas Tran Blaha 2009 lattice constants PBE Si Ge C Al Cu zero-point corrected experimental table","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01WtuoBADN3E1uBxMq2rDMVE","content":[{"title":"On the accuracy of commonly used density functional approximations in determining the elastic constants of insulators and semiconductors","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1505.02379"},{"title":"Assessing the Performance of Recent Density Functionals for Bulk Solids","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/0903.4037"},{"title":"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050"},{"title":"Lattice dynamics and structural phase stability of group-IV elemental solids with the r$^2$SCAN functional","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11177"},{"title":"Self-consistent Hessian-level meta-generalized gradient approximation","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.07046"},{"title":"calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal 47and4fs7z","url":"https://typeset.io/pdf/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-47and4fs7z.pdf"},{"title":"Calculation of the lattice constant of solids with semilocal functionals","url":"https://www.bohrium.com/paper-details/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals/811872347643445248-529"},{"title":"Dissemin is shutting down on January 1st, 2025","url":"https://dissem.in/p/27732682/calculation-of-the-lattice-constant-of-solids-with-semilocal-functionals"},{"title":"dft pernot2015 jp509980w supp","url":"https://iris.unito.it/retrieve/e27ce428-98c2-2581-e053-d805fe0acbaa/dft_pernot2015_jp509980w_supp.pdf"},{"title":"nih.gov","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3331871"}]},"I couldn't retrieve the original 2009 Haas, Tran, and Blaha table itself, so the numbers below come from later papers that reuse or compare against it.\n\n- **Methodology:** A summary of the paper says the experimental lattice constants were zero-point anharmonic expansion (ZPAE) corrected before the statistical analysis Experimental lattice constants were ZPAE - corrected, and statistical quantities like mean error, mean absolute error, etc., were used for result analysis.\n- **Al and Cu:** The summary notes that for Al, most functionals except PBE yield accurate lattice constants. I did not find the specific Al or Cu PBE values from the 2009 table.\n- **Si, Ge, C (ZPE-corrected experiment vs. PBE):** A 2026 r²SCAN paper lists PBE values of 3.571 Å (C), 5.472 Å (Si), and 5.765 Å (Ge), alongside an \"ExptZPE\" column of 3.553, 5.421, and 5.644 Å. Its uncorrected experimental values are 3.567, 5.430, and 5.652 Å. | С | 3.571 | 3.558 | 3.551 | 3.545 | 3.553 | 3.567 | | Si | 5.472 | 5.447 | 5.438 | 5.442 | 5.421 | 5.430 | | Ge | 5.765 | 5.689 | 5.678 | 5.686 | 5.644 | 5.652 | This paper's PBE and experimental values may not match the 2009 table exactly.\n- **Al and Cu (PBE vs. experiment):** A 2026 Hessian-level meta-GGA paper lists PBE at 4.042 Å for Al and 3.642 Å for Cu, against experimental values of 4.018 Å and 3.595 Å. | Al | 4.042 | 4.019 | 4.049 | 4.067 | 4.011 | 4.052 | 4.018 | Its experimental reference may differ from the one used in 2009.\n- **Other reference data:** A 2009 Csonka et al. paper reports ZPAE-corrected experimental values, including Si 5.403 Å and Ge 5.623 Å, and it cites the Haas paper's data via its low-temperature and ZPAE-corrected sources. The rest are based on room temperature values from Ref. 60 (C, Si, SiC, Ge, GaAs, NaF, LiF, MgO) and Ref. 57 (LiCl), corrected to the T = 0 limit using the thermal expansion from Ref. 58.\n\nFor the exact 2009 table, the original paper in Phys. Rev. B 79, 085104 is the best source."],"durationSeconds":7.483111059999995,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Q7qw5R6x2hkEfWmSfwWvAs","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config"