{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb46V4fXkZKHZToWky2r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: a₀ = 5.466 ± 0.006 Å** for diamond-cubic Si, from an equation-of-state fit with MACE-MPA-0. This is a 0 K value with no zero-point or thermal expansion included. It sits about 0.65% above the measured value, as expected from the DFT method the model was trained on (PBE).\n\n**Premise check (passed).** The 0 K lattice constant of a perfect crystal is well defined for this kind of calculation. No dynamics are involved, so run length doesn't matter. Cell size doesn't either: energy per atom was identical for 8-, 64- and 216-atom cells. Si is within the model's training domain. The model can only reproduce PBE-level results, so the number carries the known PBE tendency to overestimate lattice constants.\n\n**Method.** I computed energy against volume for the 8-atom cubic cell on the GPU and fitted a third-order Birch–Murnaghan curve to 15 points within ±5% of the starting lattice constant. This gives a₀ = 5.4660 Å and a bulk modulus of 88.4 GPa. The calculation used ASE with mace-torch inside the ALCHEMI environment, not ALCHEMI's own `MACEWrapper`; it's recorded that way on the board.\n\n**Validation.** I reran the same setup on other crystals and compared with published DFT and measured values:\n\n| Crystal | This model (Å) | Published PBE (Å) | Measured (Å) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Si | 5.466 | 5.472 | 5.4310 (22.5 °C); ~5.421 with zero-point vibration removed |\n| Al | 4.037 | 4.042 | 4.018 |\n| Cu | 3.639 | 3.642 | 3.595 |\n| C (diamond) | 3.578 | 3.571 | 3.567 |\n| Ge | 5.720 | 5.765 (0.8% off) | 5.652 |\n\n- The model reproduces published PBE values within 0.2% for Si, Al, Cu and C. Si is part of the model's training data, so Al, Cu and C are the independent checks.\n- The calibration file has no lattice-constant records, so none of these references came from it.\n- The PBE values come from tables in two recent arXiv papers. I only saw those tables as quoted in search results and did not open the original 2009 benchmark paper (Haas, Tran and Blaha).\n\n**Uncertainty (±0.006 Å) covers:**\n- the spread across fit forms (Birch–Murnaghan, Vinet, Murnaghan) and fit windows (±3%, ±5%, ±8%): 5.4654–5.4672 Å;\n- agreement with a stress-based cell relaxation (5.4674 Å); the model's stress at the fitted a₀ is zero;\n- the model's offset from PBE for Si (5.466 vs 5.472 Å).\n\nIt does **not** cover the PBE method's own error against experiment. That error is about +0.035 Å compared with the CODATA value of 5.431020511(89) Å at 22.5 °C, and about +0.045 Å compared with the zero-point-corrected value. Zero-point and thermal expansion are left out.\n\n**Board entries:**\n- candidate `93c921e8` (fulfills need #0)\n- `property_result` `6fc6d990`: lattice_constant = 5.466 Å, no flags; the caveats are in the note\n- checks `e5c4146a` (premise check) and `fd88d1d3` (literature comparison)\n\nScripts and raw fits are in `/work/scripts/`.\n\nSources:\n- [NIST CODATA 2018 X-ray values (Si lattice parameter)](https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf)\n- [arXiv:2601.11177 (PBE and measured values for C/Si/Ge)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11177)\n- [arXiv:2604.07046 (PBE and measured values for Al/Cu)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.07046)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":411,"cache_read_input_tokens":41670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":411,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e00040a-3162-495e-9b5a-0a2f05274bea","uuid":"48820d77-ec42-476a-a3a8-268f23516842","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:50:51.708Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb46UfcT3gXLQKR3bRx8"}
